/**
 * Sends renewal-due reminders to the owners of the given opportunities.
 *
 * All messages are prepared first and delivered with a single Messaging.sendEmail
 * call: Apex allows only 10 sendEmail invocations per transaction, so a
 * "one call per opportunity" design cannot survive a batch of 200 opportunities
 * (the 11th call throws an uncatchable LimitException). Per-message failure
 * isolation is preserved with allOrNothing = false and by inspecting every
 * Messaging.SendEmailResult.
 */
public with sharing class RenewalReminderService {

    private static final String SUBJECT_PREFIX = 'Renewal due: ';

    /**
     * Emails each opportunity owner that a renewal is due.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds Ids of the opportunities whose renewal is due (up to 200 per call).
     * @return the number of emails that were sent successfully.
     */
    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {

        Integer sent = 0;

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return sent;
        }

        // Collect the ids, ignoring nulls (the Set also de-duplicates).
        Set<Id> idsToQuery = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id opportunityId : opportunityIds) {
            if (opportunityId != null) {
                idsToQuery.add(opportunityId);
            }
        }
        if (idsToQuery.isEmpty()) {
            return sent;
        }

        // Single bulk-safe, read-only query for the whole call.
        Map<Id, Opportunity> opportunitiesById = new Map<Id, Opportunity>([
            SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :idsToQuery
        ]);

        List<Messaging.Email> emails = new List<Messaging.Email>();
        Set<Id> processed = new Set<Id>();

        for (Id opportunityId : opportunityIds) {
            if (opportunityId == null || processed.contains(opportunityId)) {
                continue;
            }
            processed.add(opportunityId);

            Opportunity opp = opportunitiesById.get(opportunityId);
            if (opp == null) {
                // Not visible to the running user, or deleted since the job selected it.
                continue;
            }

            // setTargetObjectId() only accepts User, Contact or Lead ids.
            if (opp.OwnerId == null || opp.OwnerId.getSObjectType() != User.SObjectType) {
                System.debug(LoggingLevel.WARN, 'Skipping ' + opp.Id + ': owner is not a user.');
                continue;
            }

            Messaging.SingleEmailMessage mail = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
            mail.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId); // internal send: no external-email allocation
            mail.setSaveAsActivity(false);       // required when the target is a User
            mail.setSubject(SUBJECT_PREFIX + opp.Name);
            mail.setPlainTextBody(buildBody(opp));

            emails.add(mail);
        }

        if (emails.isEmpty()) {
            return sent;
        }

        // One invocation for the whole batch (only 10 sendEmail calls are allowed
        // per transaction). allOrNothing = false: a bad message is reported in its
        // own SendEmailResult and the remaining messages are still delivered.
        Messaging.SendEmailResult[] results = Messaging.sendEmail(emails, false);

        for (Messaging.SendEmailResult result : results) {
            if (result.isSuccess()) {
                sent++;
            } else {
                for (Messaging.SendEmailError error : result.getErrors()) {
                    System.debug(
                        LoggingLevel.WARN,
                        'Renewal reminder was not sent: ' + error.getMessage()
                    );
                }
            }
        }

        return sent;
    }

    private static String buildBody(Opportunity opp) {
        String closeDateText = 'not set';
        if (opp.CloseDate != null) {
            // Unambiguous ISO value plus the recipient's locale format.
            closeDateText = String.valueOf(opp.CloseDate) + ' (' + opp.CloseDate.format() + ')';
        }

        return 'Hello,\n\n'
             + 'The renewal for "' + opp.Name + '" is due.\n'
             + 'Close date: ' + closeDateText + '\n\n'
             + 'Please review the opportunity before the close date.\n';
    }
}
```

Why parts of the approach are a bad idea

1. **`Messaging.sendEmail` inside the loop is the core problem — it is a hard governor limit.** Apex allows only **10 `sendEmail` invocations per transaction**. With 200 opportunities the 11th call raises `System.LimitException: Too many Email Invocations: 11`, which is **uncatchable**, so no `try/catch` can rescue it. Worse, email sending is **not transactional**: the first messages have already gone out and cannot be rolled back, so a rerun double-reminds those owners. The fix is to prepare every `SingleEmailMessage` and make **one** `Messaging.sendEmail(list, false)` call (there is no cap on the number of messages in that list). If you genuinely need one call per opportunity, the job must split the work across transactions (chained Queueables, ≤10 sends each).
2. **"One call per opportunity" does not actually buy failure isolation — `allOrNothing` does.** `Messaging.sendEmail` defaults to `allOrNothing = true`, so a single failing message throws `EmailException` and aborts the run. Passing `false` and checking each `SendEmailResult.isSuccess()` gives exactly the isolation the per-loop design was aiming for, at one tenth of the limit cost (in fact, one invocation).
3. **`setTargetObjectId` only accepts User, Contact or Lead.** A null/queue owner throws, so those records are skipped rather than taking down the batch. Emails to inactive users can also fail — filter `User.IsActive = true` up front if your org deactivates leavers' accounts.
4. **`setSaveAsActivity(false)` is mandatory for a User target** (a Task's `WhoId` cannot be a User). Side effect: no audit trail and no idempotency signal. If you want history — and protection against double-reminding — create a Task with `WhatId = opp.Id` yourself or stamp a `Last Renewal Reminder Sent` field.
5. **Not idempotent.** A retried or rescheduled job re-sends the same reminders; nothing records what was already sent. Consider filtering on a "last reminder" timestamp.
6. **Sender identity.** Messages go out as whoever scheduled the job, signature included; use `setOrgWideEmailAddressId(...)` for a consistent, recognisable "from" address.
7. **Allocation claim is right.** Sending to a User Id is an internal send and does not consume the org's 5,000/day external single-email allocation (200/week is nowhere near it anyway). The limit that does bite is the per-transaction invocation limit. If you ever switch to `setToAddresses`, those recipients count as external against the daily limit.
8. Minor: `CloseDate` is shown in ISO form and in the running user's locale; duplicate ids in the input are de-duplicated so an opportunity is never emailed twice in one call. In tests emails are not actually delivered but do count against `Limits.getEmailInvocations()` — assert with `Limits` deltas inside `Test.startTest()/stopTest()`.

